   "applicability": "The transferred material is a health record because it identifies one individual and concerns physical health and the provision of health care (B-HRA 2(health record)). However, neither party is a Region B covered provider. Kestrel is established in A1 and does not bill an insurer; Harrow is a Region B health care provider but patients pay it directly, it does not bill an insurer, and it is not an insurer. Harrow is also not a service agent because it keeps the information in its own clinical record for its own care, rather than acting on behalf of a covered provider. It is not a data host because it does not store records on behalf of another person. The Records Access Act supplies a conditional production-order power concerning computing providers, but no order or related current obligation is described, and the facts do not establish that Harrow provides storage, hosting, or computing services as a service.",
   "coverage_note": "I checked the Region B Health Records Act definitions, application rules, permitted-use rule, service-agent agreement rule, minimum-necessary rule, security rule, breach rule, data-host retention rule, individual-access rule, complaints, civil penalties, and regulations. I also checked the Records Access Act definitions and production-order rule. The decisive scope facts are that neither clinic is an insurer or an electronically insurer-billing provider, Harrow is not acting as a service agent, and neither clinic is a data host. Thus the Region B provisions do not create a transfer gate or ongoing duty for this proposed referral. The remaining limit is that no future Records Access Commissioner order or other future enforcement event is assessed; the untriggered conditional powers would need assessment if such an event occurred."
